  "review_conclusions": [
    "Text output wraps the payload and document kind/schema in a typed serializable envelope, sends it directly through the canonical writer and includes the final newline in its output ceiling. The source-guard assertions constrain Value materialization only in the explicitly selected text entry section.",
    "Binary output preflights the typed payload, materializes its JSON value for the existing binary codec and seals the envelope only after successful encoding. The unchanged codec and bounded-load contracts remain evidence from R018/R016.",
    "Default buffered entry points select a bounded vector sink. Streaming entry points can have observable partial bytes on sink/budget failure and do not imply rollback or cancellation of a caller-owned writer.",
    "The only public SerializationBudget field is output_bytes; internal depth, entry count, spool-file and work limits are enforced independently by the canonical writer. Error variants preserve output overflow, resource exhaustion, non-finite values and serialization failures at their actual construction paths.",
    "Document kind and schema version identify payload compatibility. Canonical reads validate their header and map serde errors through existing load errors; wire constants and public exports keep their existing contract.",
    "All24 test contracts, including binary roundtrip/header rejection, borrowed bounded strings, short writes, bounded writer failures, deep nested payloads, and the ignored large streamed fixture were read as source evidence; no Rust test was executed.",
    "The runtime archive caller supplies its explicit output ceiling, caches the seal result/error and converts the observed output failures without a newly invented rollback guarantee.",
    "Seven child wording ambiguities about fixed versus caller output ceilings, internal resource limits, traversal, serialization error sources, strict threshold excess and recursion depth were corrected only in newly inserted comments and independently recorded."
  ],
